label bait
[signal arg="bg:courtyard_cafeteria"]
Lunch break was the worst possible time for a public humiliation, which was exactly why they'd chosen it.
Han and Jack came into the courtyard cafeteria flanked by three or four hangers-on who had the look of people who'd been promised a show.
Han: Schwert. I want a proper match.
Han: Blades, rules, an actual venue, not some back-alley scuffle where you get to cheat with joint locks. Right here.
Han: Let everyone see what a rank {rank} actually is.
Lan (Default): Pass.
Han: Scared?
Lan (Smug): Bored.
A ripple of laughter went through the crowd, which did Lan no favors.
Han's face went a shade darker, and Jack stepped up with a sneer he'd clearly rehearsed.
Jack: He's not a real swordsman, that's the whole point. What he did to us wasn't skill, it was luck and dirty tricks.
Jack: There's no art to hitting someone because you got lucky twice. Real fencing takes years.
Jack: What he does is flailing until something breaks. Commoner brawling with extra steps.
Han: I heard he only beat the Princess because she wasn't trying.
Han: And that forest business, probably just got in the way of real fighters. No art to any of it.
Han: A farm boy who got lucky a few times and let everyone believe it meant something.
Lan (Mad): (No art to it.)
Lan (Sus): (I heard that in another life, from judges who scored a bout on flash instead of structure.)
Lan (Sus): (It never bothered me then, because I had trophies to answer with. I didn't expect it to still have teeth.)
Lan (Sus): (I know I'm being baited. It doesn't matter.)
- Stand up
	Lan (Default): Liesel, hold my plate.
- Finish the bite first
	Lan (Smug): One second. I'm mid-bite.
	set {aff_liesel} += 1
	Lan (Default): Liesel, hold my plate.
Liesel (Flat): Lan...
Lan (Smug): I'll be quick.
Han's grin came back, delighted, and Lan understood with perfect, retroactive clarity exactly how thoroughly he'd been played.
label first_exchanges
[signal arg="bg:courtyard_ring"]
They cleared a ring in the courtyard the way courtyards always did for this kind of thing.
Han drew a rapier and fell into a textbook Britannic stance. Lan drew a standard practice longsword from the rack.
No montante today. No reason to have brought it to lunch.
Han: First blood, or first surrender. Nothing lethal. We're not animals.
Lan (Default): Fine.
Han opened with a Shir-assisted thrust, a thin thread of gi down his blade. Competent, not dangerous.
The kind of skill you got from expensive tutors, never tested against anything that wanted to kill you.
[signal arg="combat:duel_han_phase1"]
Lan (Combat): (Give ground for three exchanges. Read the rhythm. Then stop giving ground.)
He caught the fourth thrust on a Zwerchhau, a cross-cut that redirected the point wide of his ribs, and closed the distance in the same motion.
Han's confident footwork faltered. A murmur went up from the crowd.
Lan (Smug): (Annoyed, not struggling. There's a difference, and I'm making sure it shows.)
He pushed Han back another two steps, and something small and pale fell out of the noble's jacket and skittered across the flagstones between them.
A small, unmarked white pill.
Lan (Sus): (That's not supposed to exist yet.)
Han scooped it up and swallowed it whole.
label three_days
[signal arg="interlude:on"]
[signal arg="bg:old_stables"]
THREE DAYS EARLIER. A disused storage room behind the old stables, no windows, one lantern.
A figure with a hood low enough that neither Han nor Jack could have described one feature of the face beneath it afterward.
Hooded: You're upset. A commoner humiliated you. Twice, now, if you count the princess intervening. That must sit badly with someone of your standing.
Jack: Who are you?
Hooded: Someone who'd rather see the two of you win than lose. There's a man named Kliam.
Hooded: He operates out of the lower district, and he sells compounds that borrow gi from a source other than your own body.
Hooded: Enough of an edge to make sure the next time you meet your commoner problem, the outcome goes the way it should have gone the first time.
Hooded: Give him this name. He'll know what it means.
Han unfolded the slip of paper. Two letters, nothing more. P.E.
Han: That's supposed to mean something?
Hooded: It will. Enjoy your match, gentlemen.
[signal arg="interlude:off"]
label pill
[signal arg="bg:courtyard_ring"]
The change didn't happen slowly. One moment Han was a boy in retreat.
The next his stance snapped straight, the thread of shir along his blade thickened into a rope, and his eyes went glassy and overbright.
Han: Now. Let's see your art.
He came in fast enough that the crowd gasped, and Lan's easy guard nearly cost him an eye.
Lan (Combat): (Okay. Real fight now.)
[signal arg="combat:duel_han_phase2"]
Han's borrowed strength was a river with no banks, fast and heavy.
Lan took a cut along his forearm learning how much respect it deserved. The answer was a lot.
Lan (Combat): (Don't match it. Redirect it. Let his own overcommitted lunges carry him past his guard until he's gassing himself on momentum he can't control.)
Two more cuts, one along the ribs, one across the knuckles.
Then, on Han's eleventh lunge, Lan stepped inside the reach of the rapier entirely and put the flat of the longsword across his wrist.
The rapier hit the flagstones a full second before Han did. The borrowed strength guttered out of him like a candle pinched between two fingers.
The courtyard had gone completely silent.
label after
Nobody had ever watched a rank {rank} fight a rank five who was very publicly, if nobody quite understood how, fighting with something extra, and win anyway, bleeding from three places.
Liesel (Flat): You're an idiot.
Lan (Default): I'm aware.
Liesel (Default): He said three sentences and you threw your entire plan away. What did he say that mattered that much?
Lan (Sus): (No art to it. A farm boy who got lucky.)
Lan (Default): Nothing important. Just noise that landed wrong.
Liesel gave him a long look that said she didn't believe him and had decided, for now, to let it go.
Lan (Sus): (That pill wasn't supposed to exist. Kliam wasn't supposed to be connected to a noble brat this early.)
Lan (Sus): (Something's already moving that I don't have a script for, and I don't know whose hand is on it.)
label leonora
[signal arg="bg:records_office"]
Across the academy, in a records office that smelled of old parchment and colder ambition,
Leonora von Rosendael sat with three incident reports from the private forest, cross-referencing timestamps against duty rosters.
She found the gap on her fourth pass.
A window forty minutes wide, unaccounted for on the exact day of the ambush, in the exact section of forest that mattered.
She did not yet know whose signature belonged on the authorization that had created it.
She intended to find out.
[signal arg="chapter_end:ch10"]
[end_timeline]
